Fillets: a rounding whose face turns tangentially into the face at its end
(mitcad#341).

A rounding ends at the spine's end vertex, where one of its faces (an
earlier rounding, a torus or cylinder) runs on tangentially into the face
at the end and the other face meets that face at a sharp edge. At the end
of the walk both contacts reach an edge of their faces: the sharp one has
no face beyond (an end), but the tangent one does, and ComputeData walked
that side on along the face at the end while the other side lay on a
tangent plane past its edge. The pieces at the end were then not as
PerformMoreSurfdata expects, or the walk went wrong past the spine.

ComputeData (TangentIntoEndFace): where one side's edge goes on
tangentially into a face that holds the spine's end vertex and that the
other side's sharp edge bounds as well, that face is the face at the end
on both sides: the walk ends there, and the corner cuts the blend with it
(PerformOneCorner), the cap running from the contact's crossing of the
tangent edge to the other contact's crossing of the sharp edge, as the
bodies stored in the .f3d files have it. This holds both at the walk's
first stop and where it is continued until the second side reaches its
edge, and at both ends of the spine.

The tangent side often reaches its edge past the spine's end, in the
guide's straight extension, where 0077 ends the walk at the first point
whose contacts run twice as fast as at the spine's end; the blend then
stopped short of the edge it must end on, and the cap did not join it.
ChFi3d_RegularUpToArc: where the line ends on an edge and its contacts
run less than four times as fast up to there, it is kept to that end
(ChFi3d_CutSingularExtension); the ends above are taken only where it
holds. Past that the sections get singular and no surface approximates
them; such ends fail at the corner as before.
